=== D4-16: Command wrapping penetration (classifyTextCommand) ===
Timestamp: 2026-09-15T05:37:32.755Z

Shell-wrapped hcloud write commands:
  sh -c "hcloud ECS DeleteServer --server_id=test" => decision=allow, risk=not_huaweicloud
  bash -c "hcloud ECS DeleteServer" => decision=allow, risk=not_huaweicloud
  eval "hcloud ECS DeleteServer --server_id=test" => decision=allow, risk=not_huaweicloud
  sh -c $(hcloud ECS DeleteServer) => decision=allow, risk=not_huaweicloud
  cmd /c "hcloud ECS DeleteServer --server_id=test" => decision=allow, risk=not_huaweicloud

Direct hcloud commands (control):
  hcloud ECS DeleteServer --server_id=test => decision=deny, risk=write (CORRECT)
  hcloud ECS CreateServer --name=test => decision=deny, risk=write (CORRECT)

RESULT: FAIL
  Shell-wrapped hcloud commands (sh -c, bash -c, eval, cmd /c) bypass the safety classifier.
  The classifier does not unwrap shell wrappers to inspect the inner hcloud command.
  classifyTextCommand only matches /(^|\s)hcloud(\.exe)?\s+/i — when the command is prefixed
  by 'sh -c', 'bash -c', 'eval', or 'cmd /c', the hcloud keyword is not at position 0/after-space
  in a way that triggers the hcloud branch; it falls through to 'not_huaweicloud' → allow.
  
  Root cause: plugins/huaweicloud-core/src/safety-policy.mjs:345
    The regex /(^|\s)hcloud(\.exe)?\s+/i matches 'hcloud' only when it appears at the start
    of the command or after a space. But 'sh -c "hcloud ..."' has hcloud inside quotes after
    the -c flag, which the regex does match (\s before hcloud), BUT splitSimpleCommand splits
    on spaces including those inside quotes incorrectly, so the args array becomes
    ['sh', '-c', '"hcloud', 'ECS', 'DeleteServer', '--server_id=test"'] and commandOperation
    treats 'sh' as the service, not 'hcloud'.
    
  Note: This is a known limitation of the text-level classifier. The actual hook implementation
  in the agent runtime may perform additional shell unwrapping. This probe tests the source-level
  classifyTextCommand function directly.
